 WebXR/OpenXR runtime, Gaussian-splat rendering/training, device sensors, and
 non-Coding domain adapters remain separately review-gated S3-S7 programs.
+
+#### Spatial S5 lifecycle and Construction-only S6 adapter
+
+`aura_spatial_arena.py` now owns the exact governed lifecycle:
+
+```text
+FRAME → GROUND → COMPILE_SCENE → PLAN_RENDER → PRESENT
+      → INTERACT → PROVE → DECIDE → DISSOLVE
+```
+
+It reuses `SpatialProjectionSessionManager`, render plans, the Attempt Archive, temporal persistence, Observatory projections, Action Capsules, Boundary Contracts, and read-only Arena Leases. Checkpoints are payload-minimized and assessment-only. Repeated proofs form parent-linked checkpoint chains. Dissolution requires an exact, client-reported renderer-boundary receipt: `DISPOSED` when a renderer was allocated, or `NOT_ALLOCATED` for headless/synthetic paths. The receipt binds the session, scene, and render plan, but Aura does not upgrade client evidence into an independently verified release claim. Emergency close explicitly records cleanup as unobserved rather than fabricating disposal evidence.
+
+`aura_spatial_construction.py` is a projection adapter over `ConstructionProjectState` and the existing Construction coordination packet. It validates the nested action, boundary, lease, and evaluation digest, then emits only abstract project/scope/proposal views. It does not copy Construction events, evidence payloads, people, consent records, source coordinates, or authority. Public identifiers are hashed; restricted/sensitive scenes reject floor-plan geometry; admitted project assets must be local, privacy-compatible, non-survey, and free of person-level data.
+
+The persistent Agent Bridge exposes typed Construction preparation and post-prepare Spatial lifecycle methods. JSON MCP intentionally omits Construction preparation and exposes only status, interaction, proof, decision, Observatory, restore assessment, and cleanup-bound dissolution.
+
+See `docs/AURA_SPATIAL_S5_S6_CONSTRUCTION.md`.
